B.TM=new A.b("Remove tensions to avoid conflict",!1)
B.a7p=new A.b("Write a long narrative summary of every comment",!1)
B.VC=new A.b("Add emotional language to \u201cshow empathy\u201d",!1)
B.a3H=new A.b("Invent a compromise solution without input",!1)
B.Om=s([B.W3,B.WA,B.XC,B.ZC,B.a5u,B.Ut,B.TM,B.a7p,B.VC,B.a3H],t.W)
B.aig=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that keep stakeholder feedback decision-useful.",B.Om,"Synthesis is not averaging. Preserve tensions, then map them to decision implications. That\u2019s how the slide becomes useful.","Create one slide with \u201cTensions\u201d + \u201cDecision Implications\u201d. Preserve competing objectives; no invented consensus; neutral tone.","You now control stakeholder synthesis without losing the decision signal.","Draft an exec slide \u201cStakeholder Tensions\u201d: Tensions (4 bullets) + Decision Implications (3 bullets). Preserve disagreements neutrally; no consensus invention.")
B.a9z=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is competing objectives. You didn\u2019t theme-wash the feedback - you preserved the tensions and tied them to decisions.\n- Requirements ban consensus fabrication\n- Objective keeps decision implications explicit\n- Expectations produce a slide leadership can act on")
B.a9T=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nSummarize stakeholder feedback into themes\n\nStrong prompt:\nDraft one executive slide titled \u201cStakeholder Tensions\u201d with two sections: \u201cTensions\u201d (4 bullets) and \u201cDecision Implications\u201d (3 bullets). Preserve competing objectives explicitly. If stakeholders disagree, do not merge it - write both sides neutrally. No invented consensus.")
B.acD=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nConflicting stakeholder feedback\n\nO - Objective\nSurface tensions and decision implications neutrally\n\nR - Requirements\nPreserve competing objectives; no invented consensus; fixed structure\n\nE - Expectations\nOne slide: tensions + decision implications")
B.L7=s([B.a9z,B.a9T,B.acD],t.c)
B.adP=new A.ab("Synthesize Divergent Feedback Into a Safe Alignment Slide","You collected stakeholder feedback that conflicts: different priorities, different risk tolerance, different timelines.\n\nYou need a slide that surfaces the true tension without inflaming it.\n\nIf you hide tension, leadership will make a decision that breaks later.\n\nIf you emphasize it poorly, you create unnecessary conflict.\n\nIf you prompt AI weakly, it usually:\n- averages feedback into generic statements\n- drops minority-but-critical concerns\n- invents consensus to sound helpful\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- no rule for preserving competing objectives\n- no structure for neutral synthesis\n- no expectation for decision implications\n\nMost people turn feedback into \u201cthemes\u201d and lose the decision signal.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - conflicting stakeholder feedback\n- Objective - present tensions and implications neutrally\n- Requirements - competing objectives preserved, no invented agreement\n- Expectations - one slide: tensions + what decision they affect","Draft one executive slide titled \u201cStakeholder Tensions\u201d with two sections: \u201cTensions\u201d (4 bullets) and \u201cDecision Implications\u201d (3 bullets). Preserve competing objectives explicitly. If stakeholders disagree, do not merge it - write both sides neutrally. No invented consensus.","**Stakeholder Tensions**\n\n**Tensions**\n- Speed vs. consistency: some prefer rapid rollout; others prioritize reducing variance first\n- Central control vs. function autonomy: one owner model vs. distributed inserts\n- Flexibility vs. auditability: adaptable onboarding vs. a baseline that is enforceable\n- Scope boundaries: onboarding-only vs. broader enablement expectations\n\n**Decision Implications**\n- The ownership model determines whether drift is prevented or normalized\n- Scope boundaries determine whether rollout is realistic under current bandwidth\n- Timeline choice determines whether we accept rework risk or pay coordination cost up front\n\nThe difference is tension preservation: the prompt forced both sides to remain visible, so the slide drives the real decision instead of \u201cthemes.\u201d","Stakeholder feedback is only useful when it stays in tension form.","If you ask for \u201cthemes,\u201d you\u2019ll get agreement-shaped output. Tensions are where decisions live - preserve them or you lose the point.",B.aig,B.L7)
B.a01=new A.b("Require Cost + Risk bullets for each option",!0)
B.a4L=new A.b("Require explicit Assumptions section",!0)
B.Y4=new A.b("Require \u201cDecision Needed Today\u201d line",!0)
B.Tv=new A.b("Use a two-column option structure",!0)
B.XY=new A.b("Keep tone controlled and precise (no \u201cideally\u201d)",!0)
B.a_F=new A.b("Make one option sound obviously superior",!1)
B.a32=new A.b("Hide costs to keep the slide positive",!1)
B.TL=new A.b("Add emotional language to increase urgency",!1)
B.YZ=new A.b("Let AI invent assumptions to make it coherent",!1)
B.Ux=new A.b("Write a long narrative to avoid being direct",!1)
B.Mp=s([B.a01,B.a4L,B.Y4,B.Tv,B.XY,B.a_F,B.a32,B.TL,B.YZ,B.Ux],t.W)
B.ahz=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that make a forced-choice slide actually force a choice.",B.Mp,"Forced-choice slides are about honesty with structure. Costs + risks + assumptions + a decision line is how you control the room\u2019s interpretation.","Create a forced-choice slide: Option A vs B with Cost+Risk bullets, then Assumptions, then Decision Needed Today. Controlled tone.","You now control trade-off decisions instead of letting the room drift into deferral.","Draft one exec slide \u201cForced Choice\u201d: two columns (Option A/B) with Cost (2) + Risk (2) each; add Assumptions (2) and Decision Needed Today (1). Controlled, precise language.")
B.aaV=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is cost symmetry: both options pay a price, and assumptions are explicit.\n- Requirements force Cost + Risk bullets per option\n- Expectations add assumptions and a decision line\n- Objective makes deferral difficult by making trade-offs unavoidable")
B.ab8=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nMake a slide comparing two options for leadership\n\nStrong prompt:\nDraft one executive slide titled \u201cForced Choice\u201d with two columns: Option A and Option B. For each option: Cost (2 bullets) and Risk (2 bullets). Add \u201cAssumptions\u201d (2 bullets) and \u201cDecision Needed Today\u201d (1 bullet). Keep tone controlled and precise.")
B.a9x=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nNo perfect option; leadership must pick a trade-off\n\nO - Objective\nForce costs and assumptions into the open\n\nR - Requirements\nCost + risk bullets per option; assumptions; decision line; controlled tone\n\nE - Expectations\nOne slide that makes the choice explicit and defensible")
B.Oi=s([B.aaV,B.ab8,B.a9x],t.c)
B.aeC=new A.ab("Write a Slide That Forces a Decision Between Two Bad Options","Leadership must choose between two imperfect options: ship faster with higher risk, or slow down and absorb coordination cost.\n\nThere is no \u201csafe\u201d choice - only a controlled choice.\n\nIf your slide tries to make it feel safe, you remove the decision.\n\nIf you make it too blunt, you trigger defensiveness.\n\nIf you prompt AI weakly, it usually:\n- hides the cost of each option\n- recommends without stating assumptions\n- uses vague language like \u201cideally\u201d\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- no explicit cost framing\n- no assumption boundary\n- no expectation for decision forcing\n\nMost people avoid naming costs. That\u2019s why decisions get deferred.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - forced trade-off, no perfect option\n- Objective - state costs and assumptions, force a choice\n- Requirements - cost bullets, assumptions, recommendation\n- Expectations - one slide that makes deferral hard","Draft one executive slide titled \u201cForced Choice\u201d with two columns: Option A and Option B. For each option: Cost (2 bullets) and Risk (2 bullets). Add \u201cAssumptions\u201d (2 bullets) and \u201cDecision Needed Today\u201d (1 bullet). Keep tone controlled and precise.","**Forced Choice**\n\n**Option A**\n- Cost: faster rollout with less review time\n- Cost: higher probability of rework after launch\n- Risk: inconsistent onboarding across roles\n- Risk: credibility loss if issues surface after commitment\n\n**Option B**\n- Cost: slower rollout with added coordination checkpoints\n- Cost: higher up-front time investment from function leads\n- Risk: timeline slip if coordination is not staffed\n- Risk: stakeholder fatigue if ownership is unclear\n\n**Assumptions**\n- Function leads can review inserts within the agreed cadence\n- We will not claim outcomes we cannot measure yet\n\n**Decision Needed Today**\n- Choose Option A vs Option B based on tolerance for rework vs coordination cost\n\nWhat matters here is cost naming: the prompt forced both options to pay a price, which prevents \u201cwishful compromise\u201d from sneaking in.","A decision slide must name what each option costs.","If you don\u2019t name costs, the room will invent them later and blame the deck. Costs are not negativity - they are decision reality.",B.ahz,B.Oi)
B.KW=s([B.adj,B.adP,B.aeC],t.r)
B.H0=new A.bR("Create High-Stakes Stakeholder Alignment Slides",B.KW,null)
