B.a92=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nScattered notes with conflicts and gaps\n\nO - Objective\nAnalytical question + visibility of what must be resolved\n\nR - Requirements\n[Disputed]/[Unknown] tags; no unified story invention\n\nE - Expectations\nOne question + list of disputed/unknown points")
B.LD=s([B.a9B,B.abh,B.a92],t.c)
B.adF=new A.ab("Turn scattered notes into one analytical question","You have scattered notes from multiple sources.\n\nSome are facts, some are opinions, some conflict.\n\nIf you ask AI to \u201cmake sense of it,\u201d it will merge them into one confident narrative.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - mixed notes with conflicts\n- Objective - one analytical question + conflict visibility\n- Requirements - preserve conflicting claims; tag [Disputed]/[Unknown]; no invention\n- Expectations - one question + conflict list","Turn the scattered notes into one analytical question.\n\nRequirements:\n- Preserve conflicting claims; tag them [Disputed]\n- Tag missing information as [Unknown]\n- Do not invent a unified story\n- Output:\n  1) One analytical question\n  2) 3 disputed or unknown points that must be resolved\n\nReturn only the output.","**Analytical question**\nWhat is the true driver behind the observed changes, and which disputed inputs must be validated before we draw conclusions?\n\n**Disputed/unknown points to resolve**\n- Which notes are evidence vs interpretation? [Unknown]\n- Do sources agree on timeframe and scope? [Disputed]\n- Are changes due to behaviour, measurement, or mix? [Unknown]\n\nThis is where you can see expert ambiguity control: [Disputed]/[Unknown] tags keep the analysis honest instead of \u201cclean.\u201d","The fastest analysis is the one that doesn\u2019t lie.","Clean outputs are seductive. They\u2019re also how ambiguity disappears. Force [Disputed]/[Unknown] tags so the model can\u2019t \u201chelp\u201d by rewriting reality.",B.ait,B.LD)
B.a0l=new A.b("Explicitly state: ideas are not facts",!0)
B.a1V=new A.b("Separate hypothesis from evidence",!0)
B.a5G=new A.b("Mark missing evidence as [Unknown]",!0)
B.a4K=new A.b("Require one testable question",!0)
B.Zi=new A.b("Require 3 validation steps (how to test)",!0)
B.a5m=new A.b("Ask AI to pick the best hypothesis immediately",!1)
B.U2=new A.b("Remove uncertainty to be decisive",!1)
B.TS=new A.b("Invent evidence to support the best idea",!1)
B.a0c=new A.b("Ask for solutions without testing",!1)
B.a3T=new A.b("Write a persuasive summary",!1)
B.L2=s([B.a0l,B.a1V,B.a5G,B.a4K,B.Zi,B.a5m,B.U2,B.TS,B.a0c,B.a3T],t.W)
B.aif=new A.ac("Choose the five prompt elements that turn brainstorm notes into a testable question without treating ideas as facts.",B.L2,"Your edge is refusing speculation. Validation steps stop the model from \u201cfinishing the thought\u201d with invented evidence.","Turn brainstorm notes into one testable question. Don\u2019t treat ideas as facts. Separate hypothesis vs evidence; mark missing evidence [Unknown]. Output one question + 3 validation steps.","You now convert brainstorming into decisions without letting AI hallucinate certainty.","Turn the brainstorm notes into one testable question. Do not treat ideas as facts. Separate hypothesis from evidence and mark missing evidence as [Unknown]. Output: one question plus three validation steps to test it.")
B.act=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is *hypothesis vs evidence separation*.\n- Requirements banned treating ideas as facts\n- Expectations required validation steps, not answers\n- Objective produced a question that can be tested instead of debated")
B.aa0=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nTurn these brainstorm notes into an answer\n\nStrong prompt:\nTurn notes into one testable question. Don\u2019t treat ideas as facts. Separate hypothesis from evidence (mark missing evidence [Unknown]). Output one question + 3 validation steps.")
B.a86=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nBrainstorm ideas with uncertainty\n\nO - Objective\nTurn into a testable question with a validation plan\n\nR - Requirements\nNo idea-as-fact; hypothesis vs evidence; [Unknown] for missing evidence\n\nE - Expectations\nOne question + 3 validation steps")
B.On=s([B.act,B.aa0,B.a86],t.c)
B.ad9=new A.ab("Turn brainstorm notes into a clean question","Brainstorm notes are ideas, not truths.\n\nIf you ask AI to \u201canswer\u201d brainstorm notes, you get confident speculation.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - brainstorm ideas with uncertainty\n- Objective - turn into a testable question\n- Requirements - separate hypothesis vs evidence; request experiments/validation\n- Expectations - one question + validation plan","Turn the brainstorm notes into one testable question.\n\nRequirements:\n- Do not treat ideas as facts\n- Separate hypothesis from evidence (mark evidence as [Unknown] if missing)\n- Output:\n  1) One testable question\n  2) 3 validation steps (how to test)\n\nReturn only the output.","**Testable question**\nWhich proposed hypothesis best explains the observed outcome, and what evidence would confirm or refute it?\n\n**Validation steps**\n- Define the observable outcome and metric baseline [Unknown]\n- Identify the minimal experiment or comparison needed for each hypothesis [Unknown]\n- Specify decision criteria: what evidence would change the decision [Unknown]\n\nThe difference is epistemic discipline: the prompt forces validation instead of letting brainstorm ideas masquerade as analysis.","Testability is how you turn ideas into decisions.","Brainstorms are where models sound the smartest and are the least grounded. Force validation steps or you\u2019ll get confident speculation dressed as insight.",B.aif,B.On)
B.a3R=new A.b("Preserve conflicting observations and tag [Disputed]",!0)
B.a3_=new A.b("Ban inventing a conclusion",!0)
B.TC=new A.b("Require identifying what must be resolved before concluding",!0)
B.Vu=new A.b("Require 3 resolution steps (what to validate next)",!0)
B.V_=new A.b("Return only the specified output format",!0)
B.SI=new A.b("Ask AI to pick the correct interpretation",!1)
B.a2C=new A.b("Remove disputes for clarity",!1)
B.Zd=new A.b("Invent missing evidence to break the tie",!1)
B.W7=new A.b("Request recommendations immediately",!1)
B.Ub=new A.b("Write a persuasive argument",!1)
B.Le=s([B.a3R,B.a3_,B.TC,B.Vu,B.V_,B.SI,B.a2C,B.Zd,B.W7,B.Ub],t.W)
B.aia=new A.ac("Choose the five prompt elements that keep mixed observations honest and drive a resolution plan.",B.Le,"Your advantage is resisting confidence theatre. Preserve disputes and force resolution steps so conclusions stay legitimate.","Turn mixed observations into one analytical question. Preserve conflicts and tag [Disputed]. Don\u2019t invent a conclusion. Identify what must be resolved. Output one question + 3 resolution steps.","You now control ambiguity without collapsing it into fake certainty\u2014this is expert mastery.","Turn the mixed observations into one analytical question. Preserve conflicts and tag [Disputed]. Do not invent a conclusion. Identify what must be resolved before concluding. Output: one question plus three resolution steps to validate next.")
B.abx=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is *premature-conclusion prevention*.\n- Requirements preserved conflict and banned conclusions\n- Expectations demanded a resolution plan (what to validate next)\n- Objective kept the question decision-relevant instead of \u201cinteresting\u201d")
B.a8x=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nMake a conclusion from these observations\n\nStrong prompt:\nTurn into one analytical question. Preserve conflicts and tag [Disputed]. Identify what must be resolved before concluding. Don\u2019t invent conclusions. Output one question + 3 resolution steps.")
B.aaL=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nMixed observations with partial conflict\n\nO - Objective\nA decision-relevant question that guides resolution\n\nR - Requirements\nPreserve conflict; no conclusions; identify what must be resolved\n\nE - Expectations\nOne question + 3 resolution steps")
B.Nf=s([B.abx,B.a8x,B.aaL],t.c)
B.ae9=new A.ab("Turn mixed observations into a useful question","You have mixed observations: some point one way, some another.\n\nThe temptation is to ask AI to \u201cconclude.\u201d\n\nBut conclusions without resolved conflicts are just well-written guesses.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - mixed observations with partial conflict\n- Objective - useful question that clarifies what to resolve\n- Requirements - preserve conflict, request decision-relevant resolution\n- Expectations - one question + resolution plan","Turn the mixed observations into a useful analytical question.\n\nRequirements:\n- Preserve conflicting observations; tag [Disputed]\n- Identify what must be resolved before a conclusion\n- Do not invent a conclusion\n- Output: one question + 3 resolution steps\n\nReturn only the output.","**Analytical question**\nGiven the mixed observations, what must we validate first to determine which interpretation is correct and what decision should follow?\n\n**Resolution steps**\n- List the conflicting observations and what evidence would resolve each [Disputed]\n- Identify the minimal additional data needed to break the tie [Unknown]\n- Define the decision rule: what outcome triggers which decision [Unknown]\n\nWhat matters here is decision hygiene: the prompt refuses premature conclusions and forces the resolution plan that makes a conclusion legitimate.","Good judgment is delaying conclusions until evidence exists.","If you let AI \u201cconclude,\u201d it will reward you with confidence. Don\u2019t take the bait. Force the resolution plan instead.",B.aia,B.Nf)
B.O_=s([B.adF,B.ad9,B.ae9],t.r)
B.H8=new A.bR("Turn Scattered Notes Into Analytical Questions",B.O_,null)
B.a1_=new A.b("Provide decision context (trend-reading for leadership)",!0)
B.a4n=new A.b("Require exactly one chart recommendation",!0)
B.VW=new A.b("Require a one-sentence rationale",!0)
B.Wg=new A.b("Require a short caveat",!0)
B.a1N=new A.b("Ban design advice and extra options",!0)
B.V4=new A.b("Suggest three alternative charts to choose from",!1)
B.a4M=new A.b("Add colors and styling recommendations",!1)
B.a0W=new A.b("Explain the history of line charts",!1)
B.SP=new A.b("Predict the next month based on the trend",!1)
B.a3A=new A.b("Add recommendations for improving the numbers",!1)
B.JY=s([B.a1_,B.a4n,B.VW,B.Wg,B.a1N,B.V4,B.a4M,B.a0W,B.SP,B.a3A],t.W)
B.ahC=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that make this a decision-aligned chart recommendation prompt.",B.JY,"Chart prompts feel generic unless you anchor them to the decision and force a caveat. One option, one rationale, one limitation keeps it credible.","Recommend one chart type for a leadership trend slide with one-sentence rationale and one caveat. No design advice, no extra options.","You now control chart advice quality - the caveat is what makes it credible in real meetings.","Choose one chart type to show the trend in these monthly values for a leadership slide. Give one-sentence rationale tied to the decision and one short caveat. No design tips, no extra chart options.")
B.a97=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is decision framing: chart advice is only useful when it's tied to what someone must decide.\n- Objective targets trend reading\n- Requirements force one option, one rationale, one caveat\n- Expectations keep it slide-ready and non-fluffy")
B.a8L=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nWhat chart should I use?\n\nStrong prompt:\nRecommend one chart type to show the trend in these monthly values for a leadership slide. In one sentence, explain why it best supports a trend-reading decision. Add one short caveat about what the chart will not show well. Do not add design advice or extra chart options.")
B.abL=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nChart recommendation for leadership slide\n\nO - Objective\nChoose chart that best supports trend-reading decision\n\nR - Requirements\nOne chart; one-sentence rationale; one caveat; no extra options\n\nE - Expectations\nSlide-ready guidance without fluff")
B.KP=s([B.a97,B.a8L,B.abL],t.c)
B.adv=new A.ab("Choose a Chart Type Based on the Decision, Not Preference","You need a chart recommendation for a slide. The wrong chart will make the audience see the wrong pattern.\n\nIf the chart choice is wrong, you get challenged in the meeting and lose time defending the slide.\n\nIf you prompt AI weakly, it usually:\n- recommends a generic chart\n- ignores what decision the chart supports\n- gives reasons that sound right but aren't aligned to the goal\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- no decision context\n- no constraint on output shape\n- no expectation for trade-offs\n\nMost people ask best chart type without stating what the chart is meant to prove.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - chart for a slide with a specific decision goal\n- Objective - pick the chart that best reveals the intended signal\n- Requirements - recommend one chart, one sentence rationale, and one caveat\n- Expectations - slide-ready guidance without design fluff","Recommend one chart type to show the trend in these monthly values for a leadership slide. In one sentence, explain why it best supports a trend-reading decision. Add one short caveat about what the chart will not show well. Do not add design advice or extra chart options.","Use a line chart: it makes month-to-month direction and inflection points easiest to read at a glance for trend decisions. Caveat: it won't show category composition or drivers without additional series.\n\nNotice how the answer is decision-aligned, not preference-aligned - the prompt required a caveat and banned fluff.","A chart choice without a caveat is a confidence bug.","If you don't force a caveat, you get fake certainty. Great chart recommendations include what the chart hides.",B.ahC,B.KP)
B.SJ=new A.b("Output separate Labels and Values blocks",!0)
B.WZ=new A.b("Preserve original row order",!0)
B.a4j=new A.b("Preserve label text exactly",!0)
