B.YL=new A.b("Hide the ask to avoid pressure",!1)
B.a_C=new A.b("Use vague language to sound safe",!1)
B.a3P=new A.b("Add multiple sub-bullets per label",!1)
B.a7m=new A.b("Let AI add supporting statistics freely",!1)
B.M9=s([B.a2g,B.Vb,B.a_v,B.Xg,B.a_K,B.a0O,B.YL,B.a_C,B.a3P,B.a7m],t.W)
B.ain=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that make an executive summary slide question-proof.",B.M9,"A question-proof opener is a structure problem, not a writing problem. Labels + one sentence each forces clarity.","Write one \u201cExecutive Summary\u201d slide: Point, Risk, Ask, Next. One sentence each. No invented claims.","You now control executive attention: the slide answers the room before the room asks.","Draft an \u201cExecutive Summary\u201d slide with labeled one-sentence bullets: Point, Risk, Ask, Next. Decision-ready, grounded, no invention.")
B.abE=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is \u201canswers-first\u201d structure: Point, Risk, Ask, Next - each one sentence.\n- Requirements prevent fluffy overviews\n- Objective makes the slide stand alone under questions\n- Expectations produce a deck opener that can actually lead")
B.aae=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nWrite an executive summary slide\n\nStrong prompt:\nDraft one slide titled \u201cExecutive Summary\u201d with four labeled bullets: Point, Risk, Ask, Next. Each bullet must be one sentence, decision-ready, and grounded (no invented claims).")
B.aaP=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nDeck opener for an executive room\n\nO - Objective\nQuestion-proof summary that stands alone\n\nR - Requirements\nPoint/Risk/Ask/Next labels; one sentence each; no invented claims\n\nE - Expectations\nOne slide that can lead the deck")
B.NQ=s([B.abE,B.aae,B.aaP],t.c)
B.aeg=new A.ab("Draft the One-Slide Executive Summary That Can Survive Questions","You need a single summary slide that can survive executive questions: what\u2019s the point, what\u2019s the risk, what\u2019s the ask.\n\nIf it cannot survive questions, it cannot lead the deck.\n\nIf you prompt AI weakly, it usually:\n- writes generic \u201coverview\u201d bullets\n- hides the ask\n- smooths risk into vague language\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- no requirement for question-proof structure\n- no constraints on scope\n- no expectation for decision-ready phrasing\n\nMost \u201cexecutive summaries\u201d are just introductions.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - deck opener for executive room\n- Objective - question-proof summary\n- Requirements - point/risk/ask/next structure\n- Expectations - one slide that stands alone","Draft one slide titled \u201cExecutive Summary\u201d with four labeled bullets: Point, Risk, Ask, Next. Each bullet must be one sentence, decision-ready, and grounded (no invented claims).","**Executive Summary**\n- **Point**: We are proposing a standardized onboarding baseline with function-owned inserts to reduce inconsistency without blocking role needs.\n- **Risk**: If scope and ownership are not explicit, we will ship a template that drifts and creates false alignment.\n- **Ask**: Approve the scope boundary (mandatory vs optional) and the ownership model today.\n- **Next**: Run a short pilot, validate success criteria, then scale with a fixed review cadence.\n\nWhat matters here is question-proof labeling: the prompt forced each bullet to answer the executive\u2019s first question before the room asks it.","Executive summaries must answer Point, Risk, Ask, Next.","If your executive summary can\u2019t answer \u201cwhat\u2019s the ask?\u201d in one line, it isn\u2019t a summary - it\u2019s a warm-up.",B.ain,B.NQ)
