B.a77=new A.b("Add extra background context paragraphs",!1)
B.Nw=s([B.TK,B.VT,B.a5o,B.U3,B.XO,B.a1D,B.a_J,B.a5z,B.a6u,B.a77],t.W)
B.ahH=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that keep an exec slide credible when signals conflict.",B.Nw,"Executive trust is fragile. Your prompt must prevent \u201chelpful smoothing\u201d and keep uncertainty visible in a controlled format.","Create one exec slide with Current Signal + Open Questions. Tag conflicts [Disputed], missing info [Unknown]. Do not invent dates. Keep bullet counts fixed.","You now control executive credibility when inputs don\u2019t match - that\u2019s a real advantage.","Write a single exec slide with \u201cCurrent Signal\u201d (4 bullets) + \u201cOpen Questions\u201d (3 bullets). Preserve conflict with [Disputed], mark gaps [Unknown], and do not invent dates/certainty.")
B.a82=new A.k("What Made This Work","The control point is credibility under conflict: you preserved disagreement and forced open questions.\n- Requirements stop timeline invention\n- Objective keeps decision signal visible\n- Expectations produce a slide that leadership can act on without false confidence")
B.a8z=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nMake this update consistent for executives\n\nStrong prompt:\nCreate a single executive slide with two sections: \u201cCurrent Signal\u201d (4 bullets) and \u201cOpen Questions\u201d (3 bullets). When inputs conflict, include both views and tag as [Disputed]. If information is missing, tag as [Unknown]. Do not invent dates or certainty. Keep tone controlled and decision-ready.")
B.acI=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nMulti-team status with conflicting signals\n\nO - Objective\nReconcile without inventing agreement\n\nR - Requirements\nTag conflicts [Disputed]; tag missing [Unknown]; no invented dates or certainty\n\nE - Expectations\nOne slide: Current Signal + Open Questions")
B.Md=s([B.a82,B.a8z,B.acI],t.c)
B.aee=new A.ab("Reconcile Conflicting Signals Into a Credible Exec Narrative","Different teams report different \u201ctruths\u201d: one says on-track, another flags a dependency risk, a third says scope shifted.\n\nYou must produce an executive narrative that stays credible under scrutiny.\n\nIf you over-smooth conflict, leadership will assume stability that doesn\u2019t exist.\n\nIf you prompt AI weakly, it usually:\n- collapses conflict into a single storyline\n- replaces risk with generic phrasing\n- invents a confident timeline to fill gaps\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- no rule for handling conflicting inputs\n- no requirement to surface uncertainty explicitly\n- no expectation for executive-safe wording\n\nMost people try to \u201cmake it consistent\u201d and accidentally remove the problem.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - multi-team status with conflicting signals\n- Objective - reconcile without inventing agreement\n- Requirements - preserve conflict; mark unknowns; keep decision signal\n- Expectations - one-slide narrative plus open questions","Create a single executive slide with two sections: \u201cCurrent Signal\u201d (4 bullets) and \u201cOpen Questions\u201d (3 bullets). When inputs conflict, include both views and tag as [Disputed]. If information is missing, tag as [Unknown]. Do not invent dates or certainty. Keep tone controlled and decision-ready.","**Current Signal**\n- Workstream A reports on-track against current milestone [Disputed]\n- Workstream B flags a dependency risk that could affect sequencing [Disputed]\n- Scope boundaries may have shifted; impact is not yet quantified [Unknown]\n- Net: timeline confidence is not decision-ready without a dependency checkpoint\n\n**Open Questions**\n- What is the latest dependency ETA and owner-confirmed mitigation path?\n- Has scope changed, or is this a sequencing interpretation difference?\n- What decision do we need this week: accept risk, re-sequence, or de-scope?\n\nNotice how credibility stays intact: the prompt forced [Disputed]/[Unknown] tags so the slide doesn\u2019t pretend the inputs agree.","When reality conflicts, your slide must show the conflict, not hide it.","If you ask for \u201ca unified narrative,\u201d AI will unify by deleting the disagreement. That\u2019s not alignment - it\u2019s information loss.",B.ahH,B.Md)
