B.ahI=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that balance pre-mortem and success planning.",B.MI,"Scenario planning without symmetry becomes propaganda. Mirror Requirements across futures.","Partner pilot: pre-mortem + success; 4 drivers, 3 indicators, 1 decision each; [Unknown] metrics; 30-day implications.","You can see both futures clearly\u2014most prompts only see the scary one.","For a 90-day partner channel pilot with a small partnerships team dependent on enablement quality, produce labeled PRE-MORTEM and SUCCESS scenarios. Each must list four concrete drivers, three observable indicators, and one decision you would make if that scenario were unfolding. Do not invent conversion rates or pipeline metrics\u2014use [Unknown]. Close with implications for the next 30 days.")
B.aav=new A.k("What Made This Work","The exercise stayed balanced because structure was duplicated across scenarios.\n- Context set pilot scope and dependency\n- Objective required both failure and success rigor\n- Requirements forced drivers, indicators, and decisions without fake stats\n- Expectations pulled implications into a near-term plan")
B.acq=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nWhat could go wrong?\n\nStrong prompt:\nPre-mortem + success scenario; same scaffolding (drivers/indicators/decision); [Unknown]; 30-day implications.")
B.aaE=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\n90-day partner pilot; small team; enablement dependent\n\nO - Objective\nUnderstand failure and success paths symmetrically\n\nR - Requirements\nParallel structure; observable indicators; honest unknowns\n\nE - Expectations\nLabeled sections + quarterly implication")
B.Lv=s([B.aav,B.acq,B.aaE],t.c)
B.aeH=new A.ab("Pair a pre-mortem with a success scenario","Teams plan for success and get blindsided by failure modes\u2014or plan for fear and never ship.\n\nPairing scenarios forces balance.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - initiative, timeline, and stakeholders\n- Objective - surface failure AND success mechanics with equal rigor\n- Requirements - no invented metrics; each scenario lists causes/drivers and indicators\n- Expectations - two labeled sections + \u201cimplications for this quarter\u201d","For a new partner channel pilot (90 days), write two scenarios:\n(1) Pre-mortem: it fails despite effort.\n(2) Success scenario: it works better than expected.\n\nContext: small partnerships team; depends on partner enablement quality.\n\nRequirements: each scenario must include 4 concrete drivers, 3 observable indicators, and 1 decision you would make if that scenario is unfolding. No invented conversion rates\u2014use [Unknown].\n\nExpectations: label sections PRE-MORTEM and SUCCESS; end with implications for the next 30 days.","**PRE-MORTEM**\nDrivers: weak enablement materials; unclear lead routing; partner prioritisation drift; internal sales skepticism\nIndicators: low activated partners; long time-to-first-op; partner NPS not tracked [Unknown]\nDecision: pause scale; fix enablement + routing before adding partners\n\n**SUCCESS**\nDrivers: crisp ICP match; repeatable onboarding; tight co-selling motions; fast feedback loops\nIndicators: rising qualified opps; shortening cycle in partner-sourced deals [Unknown]\nDecision: double down on top 2 partner profiles; hire temporary enablement support [Unknown]\n\n**Next 30 days**\nBuild enablement v1 + define routing metrics before recruiting more partners.\n\nBalanced scenarios are C.O.R.E. in motion: Requirements keep both sides equally specific.","Exploring alternatives means giving success the same Requirements as failure.","If your pre-mortem is vivid and your success scenario is vague, you are managing fear, not strategy.",B.ahI,B.Lv)
