B.aiK=new A.ac("C.O.R.E. skill check: choose the five elements that make this a strong prompt for a polite follow-up template when a cross-team request has gone unanswered for five business days.",B.Nv,"Follow-up templates need neutral tone rules, explicit placeholders, and a ban on accusatory language.","Draft a short follow-up template for cross-team requests unanswered after five business days\u2014placeholders for ticket ID and ask, polite tone, no blame, reusable.","Strong: tone problems here are really missing asks\u2014your picks show you can separate facts, impact, and the decision you need.","Create a concise follow-up template for requests idle five business days: ticket placeholder, clear ask, polite neutral tone, no blame, ready to paste on any thread.")
B.aam=new A.k("What Made This Work","This is what changes the output: escalations are not \u201ctone\u201d\u2014they are structured asks with risk shown cleanly.\n- Objective is a decision or resource, not a feelings report\n- Requirements ban blame language and cap boilerplate\n- Expectations force paste-ready fields so nothing inflammatory sneaks in")
B.a9H=new A.k("Weak vs Strong Prompt","Weak prompt:\nWrite an escalation email\n\nStrong prompt:\nCreate a concise escalation template individual contributors can send to a director when blocked by a dependency. Include placeholders for blocker summary, business impact, timeline risk, and the specific decision or resource needed. Keep guidance text neutral, avoid blame language, and limit boilerplate to ninety words total.")
B.a84=new A.k("C.O.R.E. Breakdown","C - Context\nIC-to-director escalation; dependency blocker\n\nO - Objective\nSecure decision or resource calmly\n\nR - Requirements\nNeutral tone; placeholders; word budget; no blame\n\nE - Expectations\nPaste-ready template")
B.Ni=s([B.aam,B.a9H,B.a84],t.c)
B.ady=new A.ab("Design a Neutral Escalation Template for Stakeholders",'You need a short template for when individual contributors escalate blockers to directors\u2014without sounding emotional or accusatory.\n\nStakeholders disagree on what "neutral" means.\n\nWeak prompts yield:\n- accusatory tone\n- vague asks\n- missing facts\n\nThis is why your prompt fails:\n- unclear Objective\n- weak Requirements\n- no defined Expectations\n\nEscalations are where tone goes toxic fast\u2014most users let the model vent because \u201cneutral\u201d was never operationalized.\n\nTo get a usable result, your prompt must define:\n- Context - escalation from IC to director; blocker on dependency\n- Objective - request decision or resource in neutral voice\n- Requirements - placeholders for impact, timeline, help needed; max ninety words of guidance text; no names of blame targets\n- Expectations - ready to paste into email',"Create a concise escalation template individual contributors can send to a director when blocked by a dependency. Include placeholders for blocker summary, business impact, timeline risk, and the specific decision or resource needed. Keep guidance text neutral, avoid blame language, and limit boilerplate to ninety words total.","Subject: Escalation \u2014 [Blocker summary]\n\nHi [Director name],\n\nI am blocked on [workstream] because [dependency summary].\n\nImpact: [customer or business impact]. Timeline risk: [date or window].\n\nI need [specific decision or resource] to proceed. If another owner is better, please redirect me.\n\nThank you,\n[Your Name]\n\nNotice how blame has nowhere to land\u2014Requirements forced facts, impact, and the ask instead of a villain.","Neutral escalations win on slots: impact, time risk, explicit ask\u2014everything else is noise that costs you credibility.","If you do not ban blame, the model will name a person\u2014then you are in HR territory instead of unblocking work.",B.aiK,B.Ni)
